  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 210 AM MDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nAfter a quiet Saturday night, the next period of impactful \nweather will arrive Sunday into Monday. An upper low over the \nPacific will attempt to phase with a northern stream low diving \ninto the Great Basin on Sunday. Subtropical moisture will advect \ninto NM ahead of the Pacific low and the approaching systems will \nprovide plenty of ascent for widespread rainfall. PWATs will climb\nto near 1 inch, which is 4 to 5 standard deviations above normal \nfor early Nov. At this time, it continue to look too stable for \nthunderstorms given moist adiabatic moisture profiles, so this \nshould initially be a persistent soaking rainfall. Unfortunately, \ngiven the very saturated soils across much of western NM, \nincreased runoff is likely. Between one half inch and two inches \nof rainfall are most likely across western NM, but localized \nareas could see upwards of 3 inches from persistent rainfall, \nespecially in favored upslope flow locations like the Tusas \nMountains. Even persistent light to moderate rainfall could cause \nflooding problems given the saturated soils. At this time, we are \nexpecting rivers to rise, including across San Juan County, but \nriver levels are expected to stay below flood stage. If any \nupstream areas receives these higher end rainfall amounts, that \ncould push rivers close to or just above minor flood stage. A \nFlood Watch has been issued for areas near and west of the ContDvd\nstarting 5am Sunday and continuing through 11am Monday.\n\nIt should be noted that locations across central and eastern NM\nwill receive rainfall as well, however, the main concern shifts \nto severe weather as opposed to flooding. The NAM and RRFS are \nsuggesting MLCAPE values up to 1000 J/kg and 0-6km bulk shear \nvalues of 40-50kt. Severe storms will be capable of all hazards \nas they quickly shift eastward along the initial Pacific front.\n\nThe subtropical moisture should shift out of the area late Sunday\nnight or early Monday morning, but moisture that was associated\nwith the northern stream low will shift into northwest NM. The low\nitself shifts over north central NM Monday morning, and an \nassociated stronger cold front will push through northwest NM. As \nthe upper low continues to slide east-southeastward Monday, the \nfront will continue to slide southeastward through the remainder \nof the area, with a backdoor front entering northeast NM Monday \nafternoon. With this cold air advection, snow levels will fall \nfrom 11-12kft to 8-9kft. More precipitation is expected along each\nfrontal boundary, but snow will be the most likely precip type \nabove 9kft in the northern mountains. Before precipitation wanes \nlate Monday, 1-4 inches of the white stuff is expected. \n\nIn addition to the rain, potential flooding, severe wx and snow, \nwinds will also become a concern Sunday and moreso Monday. On \nSunday, southwest winds of 15 to 25 mph with gusts to 35 mph will \nbe favored ahead of the initial Pacific cold front as a lee side \nsurface low deepens. On Monday, along and behind the secondary, \nstronger front, west to northwest winds of 15-25 mph with gusts to\n35 mph will be common, but winds of 25 to 35 mph with gusts to 50\nmph will be possible from near KLVS and KCQC southward to the\nSouth Central Mtns. \n\nRidging moves over the Desert Southwest Tue/Wed with quiet\nconditions expected. The upper level ridge slides east on Thursday\nas an upper low tracks northeastward across the Great Basin, but\nquiet conditions should persist across NM.",
